            {
              "id": "8e88ef37-38a5-3903-968e-9f4d2ed0b1a2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to information disclosure or even code execution if an attacker can control environment variables.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, consider using a method that does not involve environment variables to pass sensitive information. If environment variables must be used, ensure they are properly sanitized and validated before use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string value;\nconst char* tmp = std::getenv(\"FEDERATED_CLIENT_CERT_PATH\");\nif(tmp != nullptr) {\n    value = std::string(tmp);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
