            {
              "id": "fce381dd-81a4-3dc6-b3f7-7fe9fd37934a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that the source pointer may be null at the point of the memory copy operation. The verdict is supported by the classification indicating undefined behavior risk and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The source expression is a UTF-8 string literal, but the analysis could not conclusively determine that it is always non-null, which is a conservative but valid concern. There is no evidence of overlap between source and destination, and the destination is likely a local or field array, reducing some risk. However, the absence of explicit null checks for the source pointer means that if the source is ever null, this operation could cause a crash or other undefined behavior. No explicit buffer size or guard checks are present, and the count is a fixed literal, which does not mitigate the null pointer risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy operation, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Since the source in this case is a UTF-8 string literal, it should always be non-null if the code is correct. However, to prevent undefined behavior in case of future changes or unexpected conditions, add an explicit check:\n\n```cpp\nif (u8\"0E0\" != nullptr) {\n    std::memcpy(result + sign, u8\"0E0\", 3);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source is null\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you are certain that the string literal cannot be null, document this assumption clearly in the code to assist future maintainers and code reviewers.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
