              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `atoi()` is used which is a part of the C standard library. This function is used to convert a string to an integer. However, it does not handle errors well, and if the input string is not a valid integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use `std::stoi()` function which throws an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi()` with `std::stoi()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::stoi(cfg_[\"num_class\"].c_str()) > 1 && cfg_.count(\"objective\") == 0\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::stoi()`\n- `<map>` or `<unordered_map>`: for `cfg_` map container\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
